}
\description{
Products carry \code{volume_unit}, \code{inside_bark}, \code{split_scale}, and \code{round}. These fields select
a measurement procedure without changing log acceptance lengths or creating physical cuts.
Additional measurements belong in \code{report_also} in \code{\link[=merchandise]{merchandise()}} and \code{\link[=optimize_bucking]{optimize_bucking()}}.
}
\details{
\code{volume_unit} is required.

Use \code{'scribner'}, \code{'international'}, \code{'doyle'}, \code{'cubic'}, \code{'green_ton'}, or \code{'cord'}. Cubic
measurements use cubic feet for imperial calls and cubic meters for metric calls. Green
weight uses short tons for imperial calls and metric tons for metric calls.

\code{inside_bark} defaults to \code{TRUE}. It sets the bark basis for physical small and large end
diameter limits and integrated cubic volume. Board foot rules always measure inside bark.

Green weight always includes wood and attached bark. The field has no effect on those
measurements, but still controls their diameter acceptance limits. Cord uses integrated
volume on the chosen bark basis and requires \code{cord_solid_fraction} strictly between zero and
one.

\code{split_scale} defaults to \code{FALSE}. For Scribner, false selects whole-log scaling and true
selects the shorter scaling sections with taper evaluated at their ends. Sections are
measurements within a log, not additional cuts.

International always uses its prescribed short sections. Doyle measures the whole nominal
log. This flag has no effect for those or for integrated volume, green weight, and cord.

\code{round} defaults to \code{'default'}, retaining the procedure's prescribed rounding. \code{'down'}
truncates modeled scaling diameter to the whole inch. \code{'nearest'} rounds to the nearest
whole inch with halves upward.

\code{'none'} passes the modeled diameter to the procedure. Metric diameters are converted to
inches for this preprocessing. Scribner and International then apply their internal
rounding.

An already whole diameter stays whole. Integrated volume, green weight, and cord have no
single scaling diameter, so this field has no effect on their measurement.

Scaling uses the nominal body and excludes trim. Acceptance tests the physical
endpoints, including trim. Doyle retains its existing nominal-end diameter.

Located rot deductions use the physical inside-bark volume fraction. Later
percentages compound on the current net quantities. Net board feet can be
fractional after deductions even when the gross scale is rounded.
}
\section{Measurement mapping}{

Product validation uses the combinations in \code{.mc_measurement_map()}. Each row
accepts every \code{round} choice listed in the corresponding section. The preprocessing choice is
applied before the listed procedure.

\code{inside_bark} always retains its
acceptance meaning even when the measurement basis is fixed.
}

\section{Additional reporting}{

Supply \code{report_also} to measure the selected cuts by another procedure. Character values include
\code{'smalian'}, \code{'huber'}, \code{'international'}, and
\code{'doyle'}. Both evaluate the nominal log body.

Cubic reporting follows the call units. Scribner
factor approximations are additional reporting procedures only.

Named definitions use \code{name}, \code{scale_rule}, and \code{scale_bark_basis}, with
optional \code{measurement_quantity}, \code{scale_unit}, and \code{cord_solid_fraction}. Board foot quantities
use \code{'board_foot'}. Cubic quantities use \code{'cubic'}
with \code{'ft3'} or \code{'m3'}.

Green weight uses \code{'green_weight'} with
\code{'green_short_ton'} or \code{'green_metric_ton'}. Cord uses \code{'cord'}. The additional definition can
carry \code{diameter_round}, \code{length_round}, and
\code{volume_round} settings.

These fields are not product columns. Additional factor procedures are
\code{'scribner_factor_whole_40'} and
\code{'scribner_factor_split_20'}. They use the corresponding Scribner sections
and source diameter rounding, but omit Decimal C exception corrections.

The factor result rounds to whole board feet with halves upward.

Advanced \code{diameter_round} values are \code{'rule_default'}, \code{'none'},
\code{'truncate_1in'}, \code{'nearest_1in_half_up'}, \code{'nearest_0.5in_half_up'},
\code{'truncate_1cm'}, and \code{'nearest_1cm_half_up'}. Advanced \code{length_round}
values are \code{'rule_default'}, \code{'none'}, \code{'truncate_1ft'},
\code{'nearest_1ft_half_up'}, \code{'truncate_0.1m'}, and \code{'nearest_0.1m_half_up'}. Advanced
\code{volume_round} values are \code{'rule_default'}, \code{'none'},
\code{'truncate_board_foot'}, \code{'nearest_board_foot_half_up'}, and
\code{'nearest_10_board_feet_half_up'}.

A truncation rounds down to its named
increment. Integrated volume rejects dimension rounding. Cubic procedures
reject board foot output rounding.

Scribner and International retain their
prescribed length and output rounding.
}

\section{Migration}{

The previous \code{sold_by} field is accepted with a deprecation message naming
\code{volume_unit}, \code{inside_bark}, and \code{split_scale}. \code{diameter_basis} is replaced
by \code{inside_bark}. Extra measurement procedures belong in \code{report_also}.
}

\section{Status and missing values}{

Invalid measurement fields stop product validation. Missing species or
unavailable weight properties leave weight missing. Inspect \code{\link[=status_codes]{status_codes()}}
before interpreting missing quantities.

Missing measurements are not zeros.
}

